# Average length of original papers: Experimental Economics vs Psychology

Source: `data/meta-regression_study_data_heterogeneity_in_replication_projects.csv` (927 rows x 57 cols).
Unit of analysis = **unique original paper** (135 papers; identity = whitespace/case-folded title + journal + volume + issue;
verified identical partition to title-only). `length` = recorded pages of the ORIGINAL paper; int64, 0 missing, 0 zeros/negatives.
File 2 carries no length field -> no join (settled).

## TABLE 1 — HEADLINE (paper-level de-dup, all rows)
| group | n | mean | median | SD | IQR | min | max | boot 95% CI |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Experimental Economics (`ee`) | 18 | **28.00** | 27.5 | 7.37 | 6.5 | 16 | 45 | 24.83 – 31.39 |
| Psychology, headline (`rpp`) | 95 | **11.23** | 9.0 | 6.35 | 9.0 | 1 | 31 | 9.96 – 12.51 |
| Psychology pooled (`ml1+ml3+rpp`, named alternative) | 117 | **11.16** | 9.0 | 6.53 | 10.0 | 1 | 31 | 10.00 – 12.34 |

Bootstrap: nonparametric, resamples the 18 / 95 / 117 **papers** (never O/R rows) with replacement, B = 10,000,
`numpy.random.default_rng(seed=20261022)`, percentile CI; difference CI uses seed 20261023.
Degeneracy: 0/10,000 replicates drew a single paper for any group; ee yields 200 distinct replicate means
(discrete but well behaved); ee bootstrap CI [24.83, 31.39] vs t-CI [24.33, 31.67] agree.

Difference ee − rpp = **+16.77 pages** (2.49x); boot CI [13.39, 20.33]; Welch t = 9.036, df = 22.03, p = 7.29e-09;
Mann-Whitney U = 1645.5 (of 1710), p = 5.18e-10; Cohen's d = 2.57, 95% CI [1.97, 3.18]; Hedges g = 2.56;
Hodges-Lehmann shift = 17 pages; P(random ee paper longer than random rpp paper) = 0.957.
vs pooled psychology: +16.84 pages, Welch t = 9.153, df = 21.31, p = 7.84e-09; U = 2018.5, p = 3.88e-10; d = 2.53.

